---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";
import {
  astroHeadExample,
  astroToggleExample,
  darkModeExample,
  nextThemesLayoutExample,
  themeSelectExample,
  viteHeadExample,
  viteMainExample,
} from "../../../data/snippets";

const code = "mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Dark mode | Buril UI"
  description="Switch between light, dark and the system setting with next-themes, in Astro or in Vite, without a flash."
>
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl">Dark mode</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
    Buril's theme reads one class on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>. Any tool that sets it works:
    next-themes in React apps, or a few lines of script anywhere else.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">How the theme decides</h2>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={darkModeExample} lang="html" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <code>.dark</code> turns dark mode on; <code>.light</code> forces light mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      With neither class, a <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> media query follows the system, so the
      page is right even before any JavaScript runs.
    </li>
    <li>
      The theme also redefines Tailwind's <code>dark:</code> variant with the same rule, so
      <code>dark:</code> utilities always agree with the tokens.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4">
    One consequence: to force light mode on a dark system, set <code>.light</code>. Removing
    <code>.dark</code> only returns to the system setting.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Next.js with next-themes</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    next-themes stores the choice, follows the system and adds a script that sets the class before
    the page paints. Set <code>attribute="class"</code>, because by default it writes a
    <code>data-theme</code> attribute, which Buril does not read. With the class, it sets
    <code>light</code> or <code>dark</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, and for "system" the one
    that matches the system.
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={nextThemesLayoutExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4">
    A picker can be a Field and a Select. <code>useTheme</code> only knows the stored theme in the
    browser, so the picker waits until it has mounted:
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={themeSelectExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Astro</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Each React island is a separate root, so a React provider cannot reach the whole page. Use a
    small script instead. First, apply the stored choice before the page paints, at the top of
    <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. <code>is:inline</code> keeps Astro from bundling it, so it runs at
    once:
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={astroHeadExample} lang="astro" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Then a picker that sets the classes and stores the choice. "System" removes both classes and the
    stored value, so the media query decides:
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={astroToggleExample} lang="astro" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4">
    This site does the same, with the key <code>buril-theme</code> and an icon button on small
    screens.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Vite</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    next-themes is plain React, so it also works in a Vite app. In a single-page app nothing renders
    until the bundle loads, so put the same no-flash script in <code>index.html</code>; it keeps the
    page background from flashing while the bundle downloads. It reads <code>theme</code>, the key
    next-themes uses by default.
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={viteHeadExample} lang="html" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={viteMainExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">The no-flash script</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      It must run before the first paint, so it is inline at the top of <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>,
      not in a bundle.
    </li>
    <li>
      It sets <code>.dark</code> or <code>.light</code> for a stored choice and nothing for
      "system": the media query handles that case without JavaScript.
    </li>
    <li>
      It is wrapped in <code>try</code>, because reading storage can throw when site data is
      blocked.
    </li>
    <li>
      The key must match the one your toggle writes: <code>theme</code> for next-themes, or your
      own.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
